  "summary": "Building on the second critique's lock-then-tmux approach, the refined design is: (1) In `pipeline.py:tui()`, before calling `run_tui()`, check if a TUI lock already exists via `PipelineLock.check('tui')`. If no lock exists, proceed normally with `run_tui()` — no tmux detection needed. (2) If the lock exists, derive the tmux session name via `tmux_session_name()` and run `tmux has-session -t <session>`. If the session exists and we're already inside tmux (`$TMUX` is set), use `tmux switch-client -t <session>` to avoid nesting; if not inside tmux, use `subprocess.run(['tmux', 'attach', '-t', session])` (not `os.execvp`) so we retain control after detach/exit and can print a clean exit message. If the tmux session does NOT exist despite the lock being held, the TUI is running in a plain terminal — print the PID, hostname, and a helpful message ('TUI is running in another terminal, PID {pid}') and exit. (3) No config options are needed — `tmux_session_name()` is already deterministic, and auto-attach should be the unconditional default since the user explicitly ran `pipeline tui` expecting to see the TUI. The only flag worth adding is `--force` to clear a stale lock and start fresh, which is more useful than `--new`. This keeps the implementation to ~20 lines in the `tui()` command, requires no changes to `run_tui()` or the lock system, and handles all three scenarios (no existing TUI, tmux-managed TUI, plain-terminal TUI) with clear user feedback."
}
